WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.367 --> 00:00:02.702
-How are you, Malcolm?
-I'm doing well, Seth.

00:00:02.702 --> 00:00:04.337
How you doing?
-I'm doing well.

00:00:04.337 --> 00:00:06.806
Thank you for being here.
And congratulations.

00:00:06.806 --> 00:00:08.441
You opened the season
with a win.

00:00:08.441 --> 00:00:10.110
I know you've been at this
for a while.

00:00:10.110 --> 00:00:12.778
How sore are you on a Monday
after a game day?

00:00:12.778 --> 00:00:15.715
-Uh, I'm pretty sore
right now.

00:00:15.715 --> 00:00:17.350
But, you know,
you get in the cold tub,

00:00:17.350 --> 00:00:19.786
you ice up a little bit

00:00:19.786 --> 00:00:21.788
and you're back at it
tomorrow, so --

00:00:21.788 --> 00:00:23.723
but nothing out of the norm.

00:00:23.723 --> 00:00:26.192
-So last time we spoke,
you were here,

00:00:26.192 --> 00:00:29.295
you were a newly crowned
Super Bowl champion.

00:00:29.295 --> 00:00:33.566
And now we're speaking after
a game in which you played

00:00:33.566 --> 00:00:35.234
in front of no fans.

00:00:35.234 --> 00:00:37.236
How was that as an experience
for you?

00:00:37.236 --> 00:00:38.771
I mean, again, you've been
playing football

00:00:38.771 --> 00:00:40.873
for a long time, but
I'm sure you've never played

00:00:40.873 --> 00:00:42.842
in an empty stadium like that.

00:00:42.842 --> 00:00:44.377
-Yeah, it's weird.

00:00:44.377 --> 00:00:47.046
The first couple drives
felt very much like a practice

00:00:47.046 --> 00:00:48.748
or a scrimmage.

00:00:48.748 --> 00:00:51.317
They talk about having,
like, crowd noise

00:00:51.317 --> 00:00:53.153
pumped in that's
about 70 decibels.

00:00:53.153 --> 00:00:55.054
That's like about as loud as --
it sounds like somebody's,

00:00:55.054 --> 00:00:57.190
like, vacuum --
vacuuming the stadium.

00:00:57.190 --> 00:00:58.591
That's how loud it was.

00:00:58.591 --> 00:01:00.626
But you know, at the end of
the day, it was a game

00:01:00.626 --> 00:01:02.895
and you start to get into
the competitiveness of it

00:01:02.895 --> 00:01:04.497
and you get used to it.

00:01:04.497 --> 00:01:07.032
But it definitely was
a weird feeling

00:01:07.032 --> 00:01:10.136
not having
your fan base there.

00:01:10.136 --> 00:01:13.640
-I know this summer,
you had expressed some doubt,

00:01:13.640 --> 00:01:16.676
some hesitation about playing
this season.

00:01:16.676 --> 00:01:18.644
Now that you're a week into it

00:01:18.644 --> 00:01:23.015
after a very obviously weird
training camp and no preseason,

00:01:23.015 --> 00:01:25.918
how comfortable do you feel
with the protocols

00:01:25.918 --> 00:01:28.555
and safety measures
that are in place?

00:01:28.555 --> 00:01:32.091
-Yeah. I think there's
been a lot of ground

00:01:32.091 --> 00:01:35.161
that's been made up
since the spring,

00:01:35.161 --> 00:01:36.562
when we really didn't
know much about

00:01:36.562 --> 00:01:37.997
what our protocols
were going to be.

00:01:37.997 --> 00:01:39.699
A lot of it was negotiated

00:01:39.699 --> 00:01:43.202
between our player union
and the league.

00:01:43.202 --> 00:01:45.405
And I think we've gotten to
a point where, you know,

00:01:45.405 --> 00:01:48.308
guys feel comfortable,
we're getting tested every day.

00:01:48.308 --> 00:01:51.010
The positive tests have been
really, really low.

00:01:51.010 --> 00:01:52.745
So I think guys
are comfortable,

00:01:52.745 --> 00:01:56.148
but that came about through
a lot of negotiating,

00:01:56.148 --> 00:01:58.217
a lot of pushing and pulling.

00:01:58.217 --> 00:02:00.386
And you know, I'm glad
we created an environment

00:02:00.386 --> 00:02:03.456
that at least allows some guys
to feel as safe as possible

00:02:03.456 --> 00:02:05.425
as we try to play this game.

00:02:05.425 --> 00:02:07.994
-So, we also spoke
before about your foundation.

00:02:07.994 --> 00:02:10.663
And this is basically
an organization

00:02:10.663 --> 00:02:13.666
that's trying to serve
underserved communities.

00:02:13.666 --> 00:02:16.735
And in a time like this,
you know, we always find out

00:02:16.735 --> 00:02:20.139
when something along the lines
of a pandemic happens,

00:02:20.139 --> 00:02:24.110
it of course affects
underserved communities

00:02:24.110 --> 00:02:25.745
disproportionately.

00:02:25.745 --> 00:02:27.780
I assume you have to
shift your focus

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:30.850
and the way you approach
helping people out.

00:02:30.850 --> 00:02:32.485
Have you found that
to be the case

00:02:32.485 --> 00:02:34.888
during these incredibly
difficult last six,

00:02:34.888 --> 00:02:36.589
seven months?
-Yeah.

00:02:36.589 --> 00:02:38.324
I think to what you alluded,
you know,

00:02:38.324 --> 00:02:40.760
whenever you have these
national crisises,

00:02:40.760 --> 00:02:43.562
you know, often times the
communities that are already

00:02:43.562 --> 00:02:46.298
disadvantaged are --
get it the worst.

00:02:46.298 --> 00:02:50.837
And also, so does those
nonprofits that, you know,

00:02:50.837 --> 00:02:52.404
help those communities.

00:02:52.404 --> 00:02:54.440
And so, for us,
we've had to pivot

00:02:54.440 --> 00:02:56.575
not -- you know, as with social
distancing

00:02:56.575 --> 00:02:58.177
and a lot of the restrictions.

00:02:58.177 --> 00:03:00.980
We have a lot of
in-person programs.

00:03:00.980 --> 00:03:04.784
And what we did was say, "Okay,
how do we make this virtual

00:03:04.784 --> 00:03:08.587
and turn all of this into
the needs of today?"

00:03:08.587 --> 00:03:11.990
And so, we are creating
an online curriculum,

00:03:11.990 --> 00:03:16.429
an e-curriculum that kids
can go and experience for free

00:03:16.429 --> 00:03:19.298
and get job training
and job readiness,

00:03:19.298 --> 00:03:22.301
not only for kids
who are going to go off

00:03:22.301 --> 00:03:23.903
to four-year universities

00:03:23.903 --> 00:03:26.673
but also those who are going to
take those nontraditional paths

00:03:26.673 --> 00:03:28.941
and go directly
into the workforce.

00:03:28.941 --> 00:03:31.777
And so, we're doing that and
trying to not preach to kids

00:03:31.777 --> 00:03:33.613
at what they should be,
but really give them

00:03:33.613 --> 00:03:35.982
opportunities to see
other people

00:03:35.982 --> 00:03:38.617
who have taken these paths
and have been successful,

00:03:38.617 --> 00:03:41.520
so they can see themselves
as creatives,

00:03:41.520 --> 00:03:44.390
see themselves
as entrepreneurs,

00:03:44.390 --> 00:03:49.195
and really pursue
those types of paths.

00:03:49.195 --> 00:03:50.896
-You're also a producer now,

00:03:50.896 --> 00:03:53.098
and one of the things
you've produced

00:03:53.098 --> 00:03:56.903
is this really eye-opening
documentary that I think

00:03:56.903 --> 00:03:58.737
allows voices that you're
not used to hearing

00:03:58.737 --> 00:04:00.306
tell their stories.

00:04:00.306 --> 00:04:04.243
You were originally supposed
to be just one of the subjects

00:04:04.243 --> 00:04:05.644
in this documentary.

00:04:05.644 --> 00:04:07.746
And can you talk about
the path from that

00:04:07.746 --> 00:04:10.816
to ending up
on the producing side of it?

00:04:10.816 --> 00:04:12.918
-Yeah. I mean, the film --

00:04:12.918 --> 00:04:14.686
I was supposed to just
be interviewed,

00:04:14.686 --> 00:04:17.923
and it was about kind of this
spectrum of black boys

00:04:17.923 --> 00:04:19.925
and their humanity.

00:04:19.925 --> 00:04:23.195
And I was intrigued because it
was directed by a white woman.

00:04:23.195 --> 00:04:26.265
And she wanted to tell the story
because at some point,

00:04:26.265 --> 00:04:28.233
she realized she had a fear
of black men

00:04:28.233 --> 00:04:29.969
that she couldn't really
explain.

00:04:29.969 --> 00:04:32.438
It wasn't taught to her,
but that she'd just kind of

00:04:32.438 --> 00:04:35.441
been conditioned by the things
that she'd seen and been --

00:04:35.441 --> 00:04:37.243
you know, how black men
are portrayed.

00:04:37.243 --> 00:04:40.346
And I think -- I've had a good
friend tell me recently

00:04:40.346 --> 00:04:42.514
when you look at situations
like George Floyd

00:04:42.514 --> 00:04:45.385
and how somebody can put
their knee on a man's neck

00:04:45.385 --> 00:04:48.654
for eight minutes --
almost nine minutes --

00:04:48.654 --> 00:04:51.090
it's because you have to
either see him --

00:04:51.090 --> 00:04:53.826
you have to see him either as
superhuman or subhuman.

00:04:53.826 --> 00:04:56.829
And often times, when we look
at how we depict black men

00:04:56.829 --> 00:04:59.298
and black boys,
it's in those two buckets.

00:04:59.298 --> 00:05:01.633
And so there is a wide range
of who we are.

00:05:01.633 --> 00:05:03.002
We're not monolithic.

00:05:03.002 --> 00:05:06.238
You know, we have creativity,
we are --

00:05:06.238 --> 00:05:08.841
we have minds
that are brilliant.

00:05:08.841 --> 00:05:10.643
We are creative.

00:05:10.643 --> 00:05:13.079
We have fears, we have joys,
we have pain.

00:05:13.079 --> 00:05:15.147
And so really just being able
to explore that,

00:05:15.147 --> 00:05:19.452
and what does it look like
as early as grade school?

00:05:19.452 --> 00:05:21.220
What do our educators look like?

00:05:21.220 --> 00:05:24.390
What are the messages that
we're sending our kids?

00:05:24.390 --> 00:05:27.593
And really just celebrating
who we are as black boys.

00:05:27.593 --> 00:05:30.596
And now, I'm really excited
to do the same

00:05:30.596 --> 00:05:32.064
kind of project with
black girls,

00:05:32.064 --> 00:05:37.769
because I think they are subject
to a unique experience

00:05:37.769 --> 00:05:39.905
that's even, you know,

00:05:39.905 --> 00:05:43.276
further kind of
in the depths than black boys.

00:05:43.276 --> 00:05:46.178
-Well, congratulations
on the documentary.

00:05:46.178 --> 00:05:48.547
You are definitely
as adept a producer

00:05:48.547 --> 00:05:51.817
as you are a football player
and an activist.

00:05:51.817 --> 00:05:54.119
There's got to be something
you're not good at, Malcolm.

00:05:54.119 --> 00:05:56.121
Maybe we can get to it
next time you're here.

00:05:56.121 --> 00:05:57.990
-[ Laughs ]
Golf. That's one of them.

00:05:57.990 --> 00:05:59.258
-Golf. All right.
Thank you.

00:05:59.258 --> 00:06:01.527
You and I, finally we have
something in common.

